items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Marrow & Co store a customer whose contact details are genuinely optional.

```ts
interface Customer {
  name: string;
  email?: string | null;   // may be missing, and may be stored as null
  postcode: string | null; // always present as a key, sometimes empty
}

function greeting(customer: Customer): string {
  // customer.email.trim()            // compile error: possibly undefined
  const email = customer.email?.trim();          // string | undefined
  if (email !== undefined && email.length > 0) {
    return `${customer.name} <${email}>`;
  }
  return customer.name;
}
```

The compiler refuses the first line, and that refusal is the feature. `?.` gets you past the absence, but notice it does not *resolve* it — you still have `string | undefined` and still have to decide what an empty result means.

Now the boundary case:

```ts
async function loadCustomer(id: string): Promise<Customer> {
  const response = await api.get(`/customers/${id}`);
  return response.json() as Customer;   // checks nothing whatsoever
}
```

Every caller now believes it has a `Customer`. If the service returns `{ name: null }`, the crash happens somewhere far away, in code the compiler declared safe.

items.promptMarkdown:
Marrow & Co print one contact line per customer.

```ts
interface Customer {
  name: string;
  email?: string | null;
  phone?: string | null;
}
```

Write `contactLine(customer: Customer): string`. Return the email if it is a non-empty string; otherwise the phone if it is a non-empty string; otherwise the exact text `no contact on file`.

An empty string is **not** a usable contact, so it must fall through to the next option — a field the import job wrote as `""` is just as absent as one that was never set.

solution.code:
interface Customer {
  name: string;
  email?: string | null;
  phone?: string | null;
}

function contactLine(customer: Customer): string {
  if (typeof customer.email === 'string' && customer.email.length > 0) {
    return customer.email;
  }
  if (typeof customer.phone === 'string' && customer.phone.length > 0) {
    return customer.phone;
  }
  return 'no contact on file';
}

grading.starterCode:
interface Customer {
  name: string;
  email?: string | null;
  phone?: string | null;
}

function contactLine(customer: Customer): string {
  return customer.email ?? 'no contact on file';
}

items.promptMarkdown:
The Marrow & Co dashboard throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'toFixed')` in production. The build is green and `strict` is on.

```ts
interface Order {
  id: string;
  totalCents: number;
}

async function loadOrder(id: string): Promise<Order> {
  const response = await client.get(`/orders/${id}`);
  return response.json() as Order;
}

async function renderTotal(id: string): Promise<string> {
  const order = await loadOrder(id);
  return (order.totalCents / 100).toFixed(2);
}
```

Why did the type checker not prevent this?

items.bodyMarkdown:
A union says a value is one of several alternatives: `string | number`, or something far more useful — a set of object shapes that each carry a literal tag.

```ts
type Payment =
  | { kind: 'card'; last4: string }
  | { kind: 'transfer'; reference: string }
  | { kind: 'cash' };
```

That is a **discriminated union**, and the `kind` field is the discriminant. Until you inspect it, TypeScript will not let you read `last4`, because a `Payment` might not have one. Once you check `payment.kind === 'card'`, the compiler narrows the type inside that branch and `last4` becomes readable. The check you would have written anyway is the thing that unlocks the properties — that is the whole trick.

Several kinds of check narrow: `typeof` for primitives, `Array.isArray`, `in` for optional members, a literal comparison on a discriminant, and a truthiness check for `null | undefined`. What does *not* narrow is a comment, a variable holding the result of a check you did earlier in a way the compiler cannot follow, or a cast.

Which brings us to **exhaustiveness**. Handle every branch of the union and, in the impossible final branch, assign the value to `never`:

```ts
function describe(payment: Payment): string {
  switch (payment.kind) {
    case 'card': return `card ending ${payment.last4}`;
    case 'transfer': return `transfer ${payment.reference}`;
    case 'cash': return 'cash';
    default: {
      const unhandled: never = payment;   // fails to compile if a case is added
      return unhandled;
    }
  }
}
```

Add a fourth payment kind and this function stops compiling, pointing at the exact place that needs a decision. Replace that `never` branch with `default: return 'other'` and the new case ships silently as "other". The difference between those two lines is the difference between a compiler that finds your work for you and one that hides it.

And `as` undoes all of it. `payment as CardPayment` does not convert anything, does not check anything, and does not fail. It simply asserts, and if you are wrong, you find out later at a line that looked safe.

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Two ways to model the same thing at Marrow & Co, and why one of them keeps producing bugs.

```ts
// Loose: every field optional, every combination expressible, none guaranteed.
interface Payment {
  kind: string;
  last4?: string;
  reference?: string;
}

function describe(payment: Payment): string {
  if (payment.kind === 'card') {
    return `card ending ${payment.last4}`;   // string | undefined — happily prints "undefined"
  }
  return 'other';
}
```

Nothing stops `{ kind: 'card' }` with no `last4`, nothing stops `{ kind: 'card', reference: 'X' }`, and nothing tells you when a new kind appears.

```ts
// Tight: each alternative carries exactly the fields it needs.
type Payment =
  | { kind: 'card'; last4: string }
  | { kind: 'transfer'; reference: string }
  | { kind: 'cash' };

function describe(payment: Payment): string {
  switch (payment.kind) {
    case 'card': return `card ending ${payment.last4}`;   // narrowed: last4 is string
    case 'transfer': return `transfer ${payment.reference}`;
    case 'cash': return 'cash';
    default: {
      const unhandled: never = payment;
      return unhandled;
    }
  }
}
```

Now an impossible combination cannot be constructed, `last4` is guaranteed where it is read, and adding `{ kind: 'voucher'; code: string }` breaks the build at `unhandled` until someone decides what a voucher should say.

solution.code:
type DeliveryEvent =
  | { kind: 'dispatched'; courier: string }
  | { kind: 'delivered'; signedBy: string }
  | { kind: 'failed'; reason: string; attempts: number };

function describeEvent(event: DeliveryEvent): string {
  switch (event.kind) {
    case 'dispatched':
      return `Dispatched with ${event.courier}`;
    case 'delivered':
      return `Delivered, signed by ${event.signedBy}`;
    case 'failed':
      return `Failed after ${event.attempts} attempt(s): ${event.reason}`;
    default: {
      const unhandled: never = event;
      return unhandled;
    }
  }
}

grading.starterCode:
type DeliveryEvent =
  | { kind: 'dispatched'; courier: string }
  | { kind: 'delivered'; signedBy: string }
  | { kind: 'failed'; reason: string; attempts: number };

function describeEvent(event: DeliveryEvent): string {
  if (event.kind === 'dispatched') {
    return `Dispatched with ${event.courier}`;
  }
  return 'Unknown event';
}

