The same helper written three ways, at Pinegate Library.

```ts
interface Loan { id: string; title: string; dueOn: string; }

// 1. Throws the type away. Every caller downstream loses checking.
function indexAny(items: any[]): any {
  const out: any = {};
  for (const item of items) out[item.id] = item;
  return out;
}

// 2. Correct, but only ever works for Loan.
function indexLoans(items: Loan[]): Record<string, Loan> {
  const out: Record<string, Loan> = {};
  for (const item of items) out[item.id] = item;
  return out;
}

// 3. Works for any shape with an id, and gives that exact shape back.
function indexById<T extends { id: string }>(items: T[]): Record<string, T> {
  const index: Record<string, T> = {};
  for (const item of items) index[item.id] = item;
  return index;
}

const loans = indexById(loanList);      // Record<string, Loan>
loans['l-1'].title;                     // checked
loans['l-1'].titel;                     // compile error, as it should be
```

Version 1 compiles and every misspelling downstream compiles too. Version 3 costs one type parameter and keeps every guarantee, in both directions: the constraint checks what comes in, and `T` carries the caller's real type back out.

solution.code:
interface Identified {
  id: string;
}

function indexById<T extends Identified>(items: T[]): Record<string, T> {
  const index: Record<string, T> = {};
  for (const item of items) {
    index[item.id] = item;
  }
  return index;
}

